{
  "dom_id": "step-q90594",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2y}).$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{tx}{2ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$",
